.TH CYPACHE 8 2026-10-30 Cypache "Cypache Manual"
.ds AD l
.nh
.SH NAME
cypache \- secure, fast HTTP server for Python
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B systemctl
.RB { start | stop | restart | reload | status }
.B cypache
.PP
.B /opt/cypache/venv/bin/python \-I
.I config.py
.SH DESCRIPTION
Cypache serves static files and Python applications over HTTP/1.1 and, with TLS, HTTP/2.
It is written in Cython on top of fastepoll, a multi\-process epoll server core:
each worker process runs an event loop and the sites' Python handlers, natively, with no CGI or FastCGI hop.
.PP
Its configuration is a Python script that builds the sites and calls
.BR serve() ,
described in
.BR cypache\-config (5).
The Python side of a site, its handlers, apps and views, is in
.BR cypache\-apps (7).
Installed as a service with
.B tools/install
(see INSTALL), it runs
.I /etc/cypache/config.py
as the
.B cypache
user, with the hardened systemd unit
.BR cypache.service ,
on ports 80 and 443.
.PP
Cypache is strict by design.
A request that a proxy or firewall in front could read differently is refused, or redirected to its one clean form;
static files are confined to their root by the kernel;
and every limit and timeout has a safe default and can't be turned off.
.SH INSTALL
As root, on Ubuntu or Debian:
.PP
.EX
git clone https://github.com/Source61/cypache && cypache/tools/install
.EE
.PP
.B tools/install
installs
.BR python3\-venv ,
.B certbot
and
.B libnghttp2\-14
from apt, the
.B cypache
user, and Cypache from PyPI in
.IR /opt/cypache/venv .
From the clone's
.I tools/
and
.IR man/ ,
it installs the unit, the configuration, the certbot deploy hook, the log rotation and the welcome page (see FILES), and the man pages in
.IR /usr/local/share/man .
It makes
.I /var/www/acme
and
.IR /var/www/sites ,
loads the kernel's
.B tls
module at once and at boot, and enables and starts the server.
It refuses to run where Cypache is installed from its Debian package, and where a directory it checks isn't root's alone (see SECURITY).
.PP
To upgrade, run it again after
.B git pull
in the clone.
It upgrades Cypache from PyPI, replaces the unit, the deploy hook, the welcome page and the man pages, and restarts the server if it runs.
The configuration and the log rotation stay as they are; changes to the unit belong in
.BR "systemctl edit cypache" .
.SH SITES
The default configuration serves each folder in
.I /var/www/sites
as a site named after its host:
.PP
.EX
/var/www/sites/example.com/public/        static files
/var/www/sites/example.com/controllers/   Python handlers, routed by path
/var/www/sites/example.com/views/         their views
/var/www/sites/example.com/example/       the site\(aqs own package
.EE
.IP \(bu 2
A folder answers for its name and for
.B www.\&
its name; one whose name starts with
.B www.\&
or is an address in brackets answers for that name alone.
.IP \(bu 2
A folder named
.BR default ,
or a symlink to a site's folder, answers for every other name, also for domains others point at the server.
Without one, a request for an unknown name gets
.BR "421 Misdirected Request" .
Until there's a site at all, a page says Cypache works.
.IP \(bu 2
A
.I lost+found
folder is no site.
Any other folder whose name isn't a host name stops the configuration with an error.
.IP \(bu 2
The sites' folders are on one Python import path, so a site's package (or module) needs a name of its own.
One that Python or an installed package has stops the configuration with an error, rather than let the site's
.B import
load the wrong module.
One that another site has too, such as a
.I serve.py
in each, is fine until something imports it: that import fails, rather than load another site's code.
.IP \(bu 2
A site is served over HTTPS as soon as its certificate is in
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name
(see HTTPS); plain HTTP then redirects there.
.PP
A first site, with a page and a handler for
.IR /news :
.PP
.EX
mkdir \-p /var/www/sites/example.com/public /var/www/sites/example.com/controllers
echo "<h1>Hello</h1>" > /var/www/sites/example.com/public/index.html
printf \(aqdef get(request):\en  return "<h1>News</h1>"\en\(aq > /var/www/sites/example.com/controllers/news.py
.EE
.SH CHANGES
A changed or new
.BR .py ,
the configuration's included, is served a second or so after it's saved.
The new code loads in a new generation of the server while the previous one keeps serving;
code that doesn't load is reported in the journal, and the previous code goes on serving.
Static files are served as they are at once, and a new site's folder, or a site's new
.IR public/ ,
.I controllers/
or
.IR views/ ,
is picked up without a restart too.
.PP
Other changes take a command:
.TP
.B systemctl restart cypache
after changing the ports or addresses the configuration listens on.
.TP
.B systemctl reload cypache
loads new certificates (SIGHUP) for new connections; open ones keep theirs.
The certbot deploy hook does this by itself.
.TP
.B systemctl status cypache
shows whether it runs.
A configuration that fails at a start is restarted again and again, ever more slowly, and its errors are in the journal:
.BR "journalctl \-u cypache" .
.SH HTTPS
Get a certificate once a site answers over plain HTTP:
.PP
.EX
certbot certonly \-\-webroot \-w /var/www/acme \-d example.com \-d www.example.com \e
  \-\-deploy\-hook /opt/cypache/tools/certbot\-deploy\-hook
.EE
.PP
Certbot's keys are readable by root only.
The deploy hook checks that certificate and key match, copies them for the
.B cypache
group into a new directory, and switches the symlink
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name
to it in one rename, so the server never reads a certificate and key from different renewals.
It keeps the previous pair and removes older ones.
Then it has Cypache reload its certificates
.RB ( "systemctl try\-reload\-or\-restart cypache" ),
or for a name's first certificate restart
.RB ( try\-restart ),
so a configuration that looks for certificates by name, like the default one, serves it.
Either leaves a stopped server alone.
Pass it per certificate as above: in certbot's
.I renewal\-hooks/deploy
it would copy every certificate's key for the
.B cypache
group, also those of other services.
.PP
Certbot runs the hook as root on every renewal, so the hook, every directory above it,
.I /etc/cypache
and the webroot must be root's and writable by no one else.
A
.B cypache
user that could change the hook would become root;
one that could change the webroot could point certbot's root\-run writes elsewhere, for example by making it a symlink.
The hook refuses to run if
.I /etc/cypache
or
.I /etc/cypache/tls
isn't root's, is a symlink, or is writable by its group or others.
It also refuses if
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name
is a real directory rather than its symlink: move such a directory away once.
.PP
Over HTTPS, every response carries
.B Strict\-Transport\-Security
for a year, and plain HTTP gets
.B 308
to the same URL over HTTPS, except certbot's challenges in
.IR /var/www/acme .
TLS is 1.2 and 1.3, with forward\-secret AEAD ciphers only, and post\-quantum hybrid key exchange where OpenSSL has it.
On Linux 6.14 and later, with the kernel's
.B tls
module
.RB ( tools/install
loads it at once and at boot), files go out with zero\-copy
.BR sendfile ()
through kernel TLS.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM\fR, \fPSIGINT
Stop: open requests get
.B shutdownTimeout
(10 seconds) to finish, idle connections close at once.
One without a request yet, or with part of a head, gets a second more: its request may be on its way.
The handler threads don't hold up the exit.
Over HTTP/2, the server sends GOAWAY, and streams already received finish.
systemd kills what's left after 20 seconds: raise
.B TimeoutStopSec=
with
.BR shutdownTimeout .
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Load the certificates anew.
.TP
.B SIGQUIT
Stop at once, without waiting for requests.
.PP
The signals go to the main process alone
.RB ( KillMode=mixed ),
the reload launcher, which passes them on to the serving generation once.
With
.BR KillMode=control\-group ,
the server would get a stop twice, and the second cuts requests off.
Processes a handler left behind are killed once the server has stopped.
A SIGINT from the terminal (Ctrl\-C) reaches the generation directly, and the launcher doesn't pass it on.
A generation that ends by itself ends the launcher at once, with its exit status.
On a stop, the ports stay open until the server is done: clients that connect meanwhile wait, and get a reset at the end.
.SH SECURITY
.IP \(bu 2
The unit runs the server as
.BR cypache ,
with no privilege but binding ports 80 and 443
.RB ( CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE ,
and
.B SocketBindAllow=
for those two ports only, so code the server runs can't take another privileged port), and
.BR NoNewPrivileges= .
A config listening on other ports changes
.BR SocketBindAllow= ,
with
.BR "systemctl edit cypache" .
.IP \(bu 2
It sees the system read\-only and no home directories, except
.I /var/www/sites
(where file permissions decide),
.I /var/log/cypache
(mode 750) and its own private
.IR /tmp ,
.I /var/tmp
and
.IR /dev/shm .
It can't see other processes, devices, or kernel tunables, modules and logs, nor set the clock or the hostname.
.IP \(bu 2
It has IPv4, IPv6 and Unix sockets only, and the system calls of
.B @system\-service
without privileged ones, io_uring, keyrings, userfaultfd, reading or writing other processes' memory
.RB ( process_vm_readv ,
.BR process_vm_writev ),
and changing priorities, scheduling or resource limits
.RB ( os.nice()
fails with
.BR EPERM ).
.IP \(bu 2
It can't make memory writable and executable
.RB ( MemoryDenyWriteExecute= ).
Python and Cypache don't need that; a library with a JIT, or ctypes callbacks, may, and then needs the line removed.
.IP \(bu 2
It lacks
.BR RestrictSUIDSGID= ,
which blocks the
.BR openat2 ()
static files are opened with; a Site's
.B root
refuses to start where
.BR openat2 ()
fails, rather than answer 404.
So code the server runs could leave a setuid or setgid file wherever it can write,
which is why the directories it writes to are mode 750: other users can't reach it there.
.IP \(bu 2
It allows 65536 open files
.RB ( LimitNOFILE= ),
enough for
.B maxConnections
up to 16368.
.IP \(bu 2
Sites can live anywhere outside
.IR /home ,
given a configuration that names them.
Code and static files belong to root and are writable by no one else, nor are the directories above them:
a
.B .py
(or a
.IR __pycache__/*.pyc )
the server's user could plant would run within a second.
.B tools/install
refuses to run where
.IR /opt ,
.IR /etc/cypache ,
.I /var/www/acme
or
.I /var/www/sites
isn't root's or is writable by others, since root writes into them (the deploy hook, certbot) or the server runs the code in them,
and warns about such a
.IR /var/www .
.IP \(bu 2
A directory an application writes to, such as one for uploads, belongs to
.B cypache
with mode 750, outside the static root and the code.
In
.I /var/www/sites
that's all it takes; anywhere else it's listed in
.B ReadWritePaths=
.RB ( "systemctl edit cypache" ),
or writes fail with
.BR EROFS .
.IP \(bu 2
In
.IR /var/www/sites ,
only file permissions keep the server from the code.
Hardening it leaves the server only the directories applications write to:
.B systemctl edit cypache
with these lines above the one it says is discarded, then
.B systemctl restart cypache
(a reload keeps the old sandbox).
The empty
.B ReadWritePaths=
drops the unit's
.IR /var/www/sites ;
the directories listed after it must exist, or the server doesn't start.
.RS
.PP
.EX
[Service]
ReadWritePaths=
ReadWritePaths=/var/www/sites/example.com/uploads
.EE
.PP
.B systemctl show cypache \-p ReadWritePaths
shows what it got.
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Importing
.B cypache
makes the process non\-dumpable
.RB ( PR_SET_DUMPABLE ),
and so the server, its workers, the reload launcher and the generations it forks.
Like any process, the server is dumpable at its start, until the configuration imports
.BR cypache ,
and with reload the launcher once more after it takes the process's place: some tens of milliseconds at each start, none at reloads.
Other processes of its user, such as a program a handler runs, can't attach to them, nor read their memory, environment or open files (request bodies, spool files) through
.IR /proc .
Debug or profile them as root.
There are no core dumps, and the server can't change its own
.I /proc/self
settings such as
.BR oom_score_adj :
use the unit's
.BR OOMScoreAdjust= .
.IP \(bu 2
Every site's code runs in the same worker processes, and those hold every site's TLS private key.
Code one site runs can read the other sites' data and keys:
sites that don't trust each other belong on separate servers, each with a Cypache of its own.
.IP \(bu 2
Slow and idle clients are cut off by timeouts and minimum rates, in both directions; see
.BR cypache\-config (5).
.SH LOGS
Each response gets a line in
.IR /var/log/cypache/access.log ,
in the combined format followed by the Host and the seconds the response took;
.BR cypache\-config (5)
describes it.
It's rotated daily and kept for 14 days.
Errors, handler tracebacks and stuck handlers go to the journal:
.BR "journalctl \-u cypache" .
.SH PYTHON LIBRARIES
The server runs the system's Python, and its venv sees the system's packages, so a site's libraries come from apt with the system's security updates:
.PP
.EX
apt install python3\-pymysql python3\-pil python3\-bcrypt python3\-magic
.EE
.PP
A library the distribution doesn't package goes into the venv, and is then updated by hand:
.PP
.EX
/opt/cypache/venv/bin/python \-I \-m pip install \fIname\fP
.EE
.PP
Without
.BR \-I ,
root would run a
.I pip.py
from the current directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/cypache/config.py
The configuration, root's (mode 644, or 640 with group
.B cypache
if it holds secrets).
Upgrades keep changes to it.
.TP
.IR /etc/cypache/tls/ name /
The certificates the deploy hook copies, as
.I fullchain.pem
and
.IR privkey.pem .
.TP
.I /var/www/sites/
The default configuration's sites.
.TP
.I /var/www/acme/
Certbot's webroot, served for
.I /.well\-known/acme\-challenge/
over plain HTTP.
.TP
.I /var/log/cypache/access.log
The access log.
.TP
.I /opt/cypache/venv/
The server, fastepoll and their Python environment.
.TP
.I /opt/cypache/tools/certbot\-deploy\-hook
The certbot deploy hook.
.TP
.I /etc/systemd/system/cypache.service
The unit; change it with
.BR "systemctl edit cypache" ,
since upgrades replace it.
.TP
.I /etc/logrotate.d/cypache
The access log's rotation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/cypache/welcome/
The page that says Cypache works, until there's a site.
.TP
.I /etc/modules\-load.d/tls.conf
Loads the kernel's
.B tls
module at boot.
.SH SEE ALSO
